"Shakti Peeth": "Shakti Peethas are sacred sites of Devi worship in Hinduism, associated with the Shakta tradition, which focuses on devotion to Shakti (the divine feminine). These sites are believed to be places where parts of the Devi Sati’s body fell to Earth, according to a widely known legend. The legend tells of Sati, consort of Bhagwan Shiv, who immolated herself in protest against her father Daksha’s disrespect. Shiv, devastated, carried her dead body and wandered in grief. To bring balance, Bhagwan Vishnu used his Sudarśana Chakra to cut Sati’s corpse into pieces. The places where these pieces fell came to be known as Shakti Peethas, literally, \"āsana\" or \"peeth\" (seat) of Shakti. There are traditionally 51 Shaktipeethas, though numbers vary across different texts (ranging from 18 to 108). Each Peetha is connected with a specific ang (body part) of Sati and a rūpa (form) of the goddess, usually revered alongside a corresponding form of Shiv. These associations are listed in scriptures like the Kalika Purana, Shiva Purana, and Tantra Chudamani. These Peethas are spread across India, Nepal, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Sri Lanka, Bhutan, and Tibet.",
"Shaktipeeth": "Shakti Peethas are sacred sites of Devi worship in Hinduism, associated with the Shakta tradition, which focuses on devotion to Shakti (the divine feminine). These sites are believed to be places where parts of the Devi Sati’s body fell to Earth, according to a widely known legend. The legend tells of Sati, consort of Bhagwan Shiv, who immolated herself in protest against her father Daksha’s disrespect. Shiv, devastated, carried her dead body and wandered in grief. To bring balance, Bhagwan Vishnu used his Sudarśana Chakra to cut Sati’s corpse into pieces. The places where these pieces fell came to be known as Shakti Peethas, literally, \"asana\" or \"peeth\" (seat) of Shakti. There are traditionally 51 Shaktipeethas, though numbers vary across different texts (ranging from 18 to 108). Each Peetha is connected with a specific ang (body part) of Sati and a rūpa (form) of the goddess, usually revered alongside a corresponding form of Shiv. These associations are listed in scriptures like the Kalika Purana, Shiva Purana, and Tantra Chudamani. These Peethas are spread across India, Nepal, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Sri Lanka, Bhutan, and Tibet.",
"Siddhakshetra": "A Siddhakshetra is a sacred place in Jainism where a Tirthankara or an accomplished Jain monk is believed to have attained moksha (spiritual liberation). These sites are considered very significant in Jainism. Notable Siddhakshetras include Shikharji in Jharkhand, Girnar in Gujarat, and Mangi-Tungi in Maharashtra. These locations are major pilgrimage centers for Jains from different sects.",
"Atishayakshetras": "Atishayakshetras are sacred places in Jainism where miraculous (atishaya) or extraordinary events related to the Tirthankaras are believed to have occurred. These may include legends of suspended icons, self-manifested images, or unusual spiritual occurrences linked to the site. One such example is Nemgiri, Parbhani district in Maharashtra, known for its large image of Parshvanatha, the 23rd Tirthankara. According to tradition, this statue was once completely suspended in the air and now rests on a single point, leaving a small gap between the base and the rest of the statue. This unique feature has earned the icon the name Antariksha Parshvanatha (“Parshvanatha in the air”).",
"Kalyanakakshetra": "Kalyanakakshetras are sacred sites in Jainism where one or more of the five major life events (called kalyanaks) of a Tirthankara are believed to have taken place. These five events are conception, birth, renunciation, attainment of omniscience, and liberation. Each site commemorates a specific stage in a Tirthankara's spiritual journey. Examples include Ayodhya, where Rishabhanatha is believed to have been born, and Champapuri, associated with Vasupujya, the 12th Tirthankara.",
"Khajuraho": "Khajuraho is a temple town in Madhya Pradesh known for its group of Hindu and Jain mandirs built between the 9th and 11th centuries by the Chandela Rajput kings. The mandirs are renowned for their intricate sculptures and are a prime example of Nagara-style architecture. Khajuraho is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a major tourist destination.",
"Manache Ganpati": "Manache Ganpati refers to five Ganesh mandals in Pune that hold traditional precedence during Ganeshotsav. These mandals are historically significant due to their association with the freedom movement and social reform activities in the 19th and 20th centuries. The mandals are Kasba Ganpati, Tambdi Jogeshwari, Guruji Talim, Tulshibaug Ganpati, and Kesari Wada Ganpati. The sequence of their visarjan (immersion) is considered culturally important.",
"Ashtavinayak": "Ashtavinayak refers to a pilgrimage circuit where yatris usually visit eight significant Ganpati Mandirs in Maharashtra, each associated with unique legends and iconographic forms. Devotees often visit all eight shrines in a specific sequence. The circuit is considered spiritually meritorious and is an important part of Ganapatya worship.",
