"Palanquin": "A palanquin is a traditional human-powered vehicle made of wood, often shaped like a rectangular or oval box. It has openings along its longer sides for ventilation and is fitted with long carrying poles running along the shorter sides (the breadth). It is designed to be carried by four or more people. Palanquins were traditionally used as a mode of transport for important individuals such as kings, gurus, and other respected persons, primarily during special occasions. Perhaps due to their association with prestige and honor, palanquins also hold ceremonial significance and are used to carry murtis of Devi and Devtas during religious processions.",
"Ghungroo": "Ghungroo are small metallic bells strung together and worn around the ankles by classical Indian dancers. They produce rhythmic sounds that accentuate the dancer’s movements and help keep time during performances. Ghungroo are an essential part of many Indian dance forms like Kathak and Bharatanatyam.",
"Yadavas": "The Yadava dynasty of Devagiri, also known as the Seuna branch, controlled a territory extending from the Narmada River in the north to the Tungabhadra River in the south. Their domain included present-day Maharashtra, northern Karnataka, and parts of Madhya Pradesh. Devagiri, now known as Daulatabad, served as one of their capitals. The Yadavas were an important medieval Deccan dynasty, and their rule lasted roughly from the 12th to early 14th century. The dynasty fell following its annexation by the Khilji dynasty of the Delhi Sultanate.",
"Rajaram Bhonsale": "Rajaram I was the third ruler of the Maratha empire. He was the second son of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and the younger half-brother of Sambhaji. His ascension to the throne in 1689 followed his brother Sambhaji's execution at the hands of Aurangzeb. Rajaram died of what is suspected to be a liver disease in 1700 at Sinhagad near Pune.",
"Rajaram Bhosale": "Rajaram I was the third ruler of the Maratha empire. He was the second son of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and the younger half-brother of Sambhaji. His ascension to the throne in 1689 followed his brother Sambhaji's execution at the hands of Aurangzeb. Rajaram died of what is suspected to be a liver disease in 1700 at Sinhagad near Pune.",
